 Re-running a ticket (crash retry, review reject) no longer overwrites its raw log. Before a new attempt writes, the ticket's existing raw logs rotate to attempt-numbered names, and the long-standing paths always hold the current attempt. Resolver logs rotate the same way and count as attempts. Every attempt of a ticket stays on disk, so the whole history of the ticket's work is preserved; the crash interrupt's log-path body and manual terminal tailing keep working untouched because the current attempt keeps the well-known path.
 
 ## Acceptance criteria
 
-- [ ] On a re-run, the ticket's existing raw log rotates to its attempt-numbered name before the new attempt writes; the resolver log rotates likewise
-- [ ] The long-standing log paths always hold the current attempt
-- [ ] Rotated filenames agree with the attempt numbers in the ticket's events file
-- [ ] The crash interrupt's log-path body still points at the live current-attempt log
-- [ ] Engine temp-pool test: a scripted re-run leaves both attempts' logs on disk under the expected names, newest at the long-standing path
-- [ ] Full test suite, typecheck, and build clean
